CAT = "rsracInstall"; SEG = "INS"
Q = [
("Which way should a horizontal suction line be pitched?",
 "Down toward the compressor", ["Up toward the compressor", "Level with no pitch at all", "Down toward the evaporator"],
 "A slight pitch toward the compressor lets oil drain with the vapour flow. Pitching it back toward the evaporator lets oil collect where it cannot return."),
("Why is a condensate drain line from an evaporator fitted with a trap?",
 "Seals out fan pressure", ["Speeds water flow", "Holds a refrigerant charge", "Keeps moisture in the pan"],
 "A blow-through or draw-through coil puts the drain pan under pressure or suction. The trap's water seal stops air moving through the drain so water can leave the pan."),
("A heat pump's outdoor unit is set on a stand in a snowy region. Why?",
 "Defrost water and snow can clear", ["It reduces line set length", "It speeds up the outdoor fan", "It prevents refrigerant migration"],
 "Raising the unit above expected snow depth keeps the coil clear and lets defrost meltwater drain away instead of freezing around the base."),
("Why are rubber isolators placed under a compressor rack?",
 "Keep vibration out of the building", ["Insulate it from the cold floor", "Ground the frame electrically", "Raise it for oil draining"],
 "Isolators absorb running vibration so it is not carried into the building. Piping also gets vibration eliminators or loops for the same reason."),
("Where should a vibration eliminator be installed on a compressor's suction line?",
 "Near it, parallel to the shaft", ["At the evaporator outlet", "Across the shaft at the far end", "Midway up the suction riser"],
 "Placing it near the compressor and parallel to the crankshaft absorbs movement where it starts. Mounted across that axis, the bellows is stressed and can crack."),
("Why is a filter-drier replaced whenever a system is opened for repair?",
 "Moisture and debris get in", ["Driers lose their oil charge", "Code requires a new colour", "It lowers the head pressure"],
 "Opening a system admits humid air and possibly debris. A new drier in the liquid line captures them, and an old drier may already be saturated."),
("Where is a liquid-line sight glass usually installed?",
 "After the filter-drier", ["Before the receiver", "On the suction line", "At the compressor discharge"],
 "Placed downstream of the drier and ahead of the metering device, the sight glass shows the liquid entering the valve and its moisture indicator shows the system's dryness."),
("When copper tubing is supported from steel hangers, why is an isolating cushion used?",
 "Stops corrosion and wear", ["Adds insulation value", "Holds the pipe level", "Lets the pipe expand freely"],
 "Copper touching steel in damp conditions corrodes by galvanic action, and vibration wears through bare contact. Cushioned clamps prevent both."),
("Why does a long copper line in a high-temperature run need room for expansion?",
 "Copper lengthens when heated", ["Copper shrinks under pressure", "Refrigerant expands the wall", "Supports sag in summer"],
 "Copper expands noticeably with temperature. Without offsets or loops, stress at fittings and anchors can crack joints."),
("A ductless head is mounted on an exterior wall. Why is the line set hole drilled sloping down to the outside?",
 "Drains to the outside", ["It shortens the line set", "It reduces refrigerant noise", "It stops the wall from cracking"],
 "A slight downward slope to the outside carries the condensate drain by gravity and keeps rain from running back into the wall."),
("New mineral-oil equipment is converted to an HFC. Why is most of the old oil removed first?",
 "Mineral oil mixes poorly with HFCs", ["Mineral oil boils in HFC systems", "POE dissolves the old oil seal", "Oil weight sets the new charge"],
 "Mineral oil is poorly miscible with HFC refrigerants, so it would not return to the compressor. Makers give a limit on residual mineral oil after changing to POE."),
("After a refrigerant retrofit, how is the system identified for the next mechanic?",
 "Retrofit label on the unit", ["Paint the lines a new colour", "Note it on the thermostat", "Tape the old nameplate over"],
 "A retrofit label shows the new refrigerant, oil type and charge so later service uses the right materials. The code of practice calls for this labelling."),
("A replacement compressor is a different model from the original. What must match the system?",
 "Capacity, voltage and refrigerant", ["Paint colour and shipping weight", "Brand of contactor", "Serial number format"],
 "A substitute must suit the system's refrigerant, operating range, capacity and electrical supply, or the system will be mismatched."),
("Why are long-radius elbows preferred to sharp fittings in refrigerant lines?",
 "Lower pressure drop", ["Easier to insulate", "Cheaper than straight tube", "Higher pressure rating"],
 "Each fitting adds equivalent length. Long-radius bends add less resistance than short elbows, which matters most on suction lines."),
("Why might a split system be shipped from the factory with a nitrogen holding charge?",
 "Seals parts until install", ["Pre-tests the compressor", "Adds part of the refrigerant charge", "Cools the coil during transport"],
 "A dry nitrogen holding charge keeps the coil or components sealed and dry until installation. The mechanic releases it before evacuation."),
("Why is a holding charge of dry nitrogen left in piping installed before the equipment arrives?",
 "Shows whether the piping stays tight", ["It replaces the leak test", "It speeds up evacuation later", "It cleans flux from the joints"],
 "Pressurized piping left in place reveals damage or leaks if the pressure drops, and keeps moisture out until the equipment is connected."),
("A rooftop unit is set on a factory roof curb. What does the curb provide?",
 "Sealed roof support", ["A refrigerant receiver", "A space for the disconnect", "An outdoor air filter"],
 "The curb raises and supports the unit, gives a sealed transition through the roof for supply and return ducts, and is flashed into the roofing."),
("Why are condensing units kept level when they are set?",
 "Compressor oil and drainage need it", ["Gauges read wrong off level", "The fan blade must be balanced", "Refrigerant only flows when level"],
 "Compressors are designed to run level so oil stays where it lubricates, and coils and pans drain properly. Makers give a level tolerance."),
("How is a liquid line routed past a warm attic in a split system?",
 "Kept apart from the suction line", ["Taped tight to the suction line", "Run through the return duct", "Laid on the attic insulation bare"],
 "Strapping the warm liquid line to the cold suction line transfers heat between them and can flash liquid or raise superheat. They are kept separate unless designed as a heat exchanger."),
("Before connecting an outdoor unit's service valves, what should be confirmed on its line set?",
 "Clean, dry, right-sized tubing", ["Matching insulation colour", "Factory coil tag still on", "Wall bracket painted to match"],
 "The line set must match the maker's sizes and be clean and dry. Reused or undersized lines are a common cause of later failures."),
("A walk-in cooler evaporator is hung from the ceiling. Why is clearance kept behind the coil?",
 "Air must reach the coil", ["It stores spare filters", "It cools the ceiling panels", "It hides the drain heater"],
 "The coil needs open space for air to enter. Blocking it starves airflow, so the coil frosts heavily and the box warms."),
("Why is a heat tape or drain line heater fitted on a freezer evaporator drain?",
 "Drain line stays clear", ["Warms the box during defrost", "Stops refrigerant migration", "Dries the insulation"],
 "In a freezer the drain line passes through sub-zero air. A heater keeps defrost water liquid until it leaves the box."),
("A new refrigerant circuit is ready to be charged, but it has not been leak tested. What must happen first?",
 "Pressure test it", ["Charge it, then use soap", "Run it to see if it holds", "Install the thermostat"],
 "Systems are leak tested before charging. Federal rules prohibit charging a system without a leak test, and charging to look for leaks wastes refrigerant."),
("When a wall penetration is made for a line set, what keeps out weather and pests?",
 "Sealed sleeve or cover", ["Extra insulation on the line", "A drain trap", "Wider wall bracket"],
 "Penetrations get a sleeve or line set cover sealed against the wall, which stops water, air leakage and insects."),
]
